 "rules": "Arbitration of passes A and B (P3/C), starting from A's runs and line labels. Settled rules applied: 1) tilde (hooked at left, wave/arc, up-tick) vs macron (crisp straight high bar; only 81/82/83 on P1370706); doubtful -> tilde. 2) dots: two dots = diaeresis, faint or single dot = diaeresis?; dot over 1 = cipher mark (diaeresis). 3) one name per shape: SYM:sigma = closed loop with long flat top stroke (б, 6-like but unlike the digit 6 of this hand); SYM:autre-sigma-petit = small loop with short tick/straight stroke, no arrowhead; SYM:mars = loop + straight diagonal with arrowhead; SYM:autre-II-barre = II between bars (? removed); other names as in the rule list. 4) reference marks, struck material and flourishes are not tokens; interlinear corrections/insertions are their own runs (L25i on P1370710, L13i on P1370711). Symbols carry no mark field: a mark above a symbol is recorded in the run note. '?' = still uncertain.",
 "notes": "P1370706 = bifolium, letter dated A Paris le 13 Aoust 1651 (right page). P1370710 (read after 90 deg clockwise rotation) + P1370711 = letter of 6 Aoust 1651 with P.S. dated 8 Aoust 1651. Clear text is A's rough anchor reading (not arbitrated except the dates). Not visually re-checked beyond the listed disagreements and naming: undisputed tokens keep A's reading (incl. its '?' flags). Open points: whether SYM:autre-sigma-petit and SYM:sigma are one sign; the alpha at the start of P1370710 L22 (kept as cipher, could still be a clear 'a' continuing the clear words of L21); P1370710 L10 23 written over 33; P1370711 L6 heavy oblique stroke over 96."
